      "properties": {
        "cnr_id": "comfy-core",
        "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "## 1920x1080 output - how it is done here\n\n6 GB of VRAM cannot *sample* at 1080p, so this graph does it in two stages:\n\n1. Generate the clip at **864 x 480** (16:9, the model's 480p comfort zone).\n2. Upscale the decoded frames **4x** with UltraSharp, then rescale to **1920 x 1080**.\n\nYou get a true 1920x1080 file. Detail is interpolated, not invented - it looks\nlike good 480p shot on a sharper lens, not like native 1080p.\n\n### If it runs out of memory (OOM)\nClick the `UpscaleModelLoader` node and press **Ctrl+B** to bypass it.\nThe graph then does a plain Lanczos 1920x1080 upscale: much less VRAM,\nslightly softer, and it never fails.\n\n### Other limits\n- 81 frames x 1080p needs a lot of RAM; 49 frames is safer.\n- Native 1080p sampling needs ~20 GB VRAM. On this laptop that means a cloud GPU\n  (RunPod / fal / Replicate, roughly 0.3-0.8 USD per clip)."
      ],
      "title": "Read me",
      "color": "#222",
      "bgcolor": "#000"
    }
